      "question": {
        ":question/id": "84c7e966-96b7-5e2c-ab4c-0d93d8c57026",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Use the linear substitution $u=3 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|3 x|=3|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{3}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{9 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9\\int \\frac{1}{|3 x|\\sqrt{(3 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 9(\\frac{1}{3})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C \\\\  &= 3 \\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(3 x \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|3 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "e0a36f36-3cca-5caf-af0f-d26099a43110",
