        ":question/id": "a8e1e0d2-fc46-5618-9a39-5e6fdde9fc87",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{4 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Use the linear substitution $u=2 x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2,\\qquad \\mathrm{d}x=(\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nThe substitution factor is positive, so the absolute values satisfy\n\n$$\n|u|=|2 x|=2|x|,\\qquad \\frac{1}{|x|}=\\frac{2}{|u|}.\n$$\n\nApply the basic inverse-trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{4 x^{2} - 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1\\int \\frac{1}{|2 x|\\sqrt{(2 x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  &= 1(\\frac{1}{2})\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^{2}-1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}+C \\\\  &= \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsec}{\\left(2 x \\right)}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative applies on either interval where $|2 x|>1$; the constant may differ between those intervals.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
